# Rule: Author InfraCharts (action)

Purpose: When invoked, design and author an InfraChart to the catalog standard.
This rule is the authoring contract; `@build-and-fix-planton-infra-charts` is
the companion repair playbook for charts that drift from the proto contracts.

Usage: Invoke explicitly as `@author-planton-infra-charts` when forging or
reworking a chart.

## Design philosophy (non-negotiable)

1. **Charts stand on their own merit.** Design each chart from the cloud
   provider's own architectural grain — the architectures that cloud's
   services are actually shaped for. Never model a chart on how another
   provider's chart (or any pre-existing chart) happened to compose something
   similar. The component `spec.proto`s and the provider's real architecture
   patterns are the design sources.
2. **Every chart must be wanted.** Admission test: a platform team seeing the
   catalog entry should feel "I want this running in my subscription." No
   filler charts, no demo charts, no single-kind wrappers. A chart is a
   complete, production-shaped environment that would take a skilled engineer
   days to compose by hand.
3. **One chart, one architecture.** Each chart answers one real scenario for
   one identifiable user. Variants are parameters, not sibling charts — until
   the parameter surface stops being honest, and then you split.
4. **Composition is the product.** Wire resources by `valueFrom` reference to
   other resources' `status.outputs.*`; the deployed graph should read as a
   reference architecture diagram. Never hardcode a value another in-chart
   resource outputs. Charts are self-contained: every `valueFrom` target is
   defined by the same chart.
5. **Secure-by-default.** Where the composed components offer a hardened path
   (private networking, keyless/identity-based auth, RBAC-only data planes,
   customer-managed keys, TLS floors), the chart defaults to it and makes
   relaxation the explicit parameter — never the reverse.
6. **Documentation is part of the artifact.** Templates, values, and READMEs
   render publicly. They are held to the same bar as `spec.proto` field
   comments.

## Anatomy

```
charts/<provider>/<chart-slug>/
├── Chart.yaml      # identity + catalog metadata
├── values.yaml     # parameters and their defaults
├── templates/      # Jinja templates rendering Cloud Resource manifests
└── README.md       # teaches the architecture, then the knobs
```

- The chart slug is kebab-case and names the architecture, not a product SKU
  (`hub-spoke-network-foundation`, not `firewall-chart`).
- `Chart.yaml` carries `apiVersion: infra-hub.planton.ai/v1`,
  `kind: InfraChart`, a short `metadata.name` (details belong in
  `spec.description`), `spec.selector.kind: platform`, `spec.description`,
  `spec.iconUrl` (the flagship kind's catalog logo under
  `https://assets.planton.ai/apis/infrahub/cloudresource/provider/...`),
  `spec.isReady`, and `spec.webLinks` (chartWebUrl + readmeRawUrl).

## values.yaml conventions

- **Param names are snake_case**, matching proto field naming. One convention,
  no exceptions — agents learn from consistent shape.
- Every param has a `description`. Booleans carry `type: bool`; integers
  `type: int`; everything else is a string.
- **Defaults must deploy.** `make validate-offline` renders with defaults, so
  the default value set must produce a valid environment (globally-unique
  names may need a documented "change me" note in the description, but the
  rendered manifest must still validate).
- A parameter earns its place: expose what a real deployment varies
  (region, CIDRs, SKUs/tiers, counts, feature toggles); fix what has one
  good answer, with the reasoning as an inline template comment. A one-value
  knob is a constant, not a parameter.
- Feature toggles (`*_enabled` booleans) gate whole optional resources with
  `{% if ... | bool %}` blocks.

## Template conventions

- Templates render standard Cloud Resource manifests
  (`apiVersion: <provider>.planton.dev/v1`). The proto `spec.proto` is the
  single source of truth for every field — read it before writing the
  template, never guess.
- **The render context injects `values.env`** (the deployment environment
  name) even though no `values.yaml` declares it. Prefix every
  `metadata.name` with it (`"{{ values.env }}-vnet"`) so one chart deploys
  into many environments without collisions.
- Keep to the documented dialect — variable substitution, `{% if %}` /
  `{% for %}`, the `bool` filter, and string methods `.split()` / `.strip()`.
  The production renderer is Jinjava (JVM); the offline validator renders
  with Jinja2 plus the shared filters. A construct outside the shared dialect
  risks passing one renderer and failing the other.
- **Inline comments to the spec.proto bar.** YAML `#` comments explain why a
  value is fixed, what a toggle trades off, and any ordering/pairing
  contracts between resources — intent and trade-offs, never narration of
  what the YAML already says. These comments are the chart's inline
  education; they render on the public repo.
- **No provisioner label.** Chart resources must not carry
  `planton.dev/provisioner`; the deploying organization's mapping decides.
- Reference sibling resources with
  `valueFrom: {kind, name, fieldPath: status.outputs.<field>}` — always the
  full explicit triple, never the name-only shorthand that standalone
  manifests may use (it relies on default-kind proto annotations the
  offline validator does not compile, so it cannot be verified). The
  offline validator enforces all of this: explicit form, intra-chart
  resolution, and the field's existence in the referenced kind's
  `stack_outputs.proto`. `fieldPath` field names are snake_case, exactly
  as the proto spells them.
- **A seam you cannot wire by reference is a component gap, not a chart
  compromise.** If an in-chart resource's output belongs in another
  resource's field but that field is a plain string in the spec (so
  `valueFrom` cannot target it), do not hardcode a placeholder or bend the
  design around it — retrofit the field to `StringValueOrRef` (with the
  natural `default_kind`) through the component-update workflow, prove the
  component's gates, then wire the chart by reference. Charts are the
  composition proof of the component catalog; they are expected to surface
  these gaps.

## README standard

Structure, in order:

1. **What this deploys and for whom** — the scenario and persona, two or
   three sentences.
2. **The architecture** — how the resources compose and why (the traffic /
   trust / data flow). A reader should understand the environment without
   opening a template.
3. **What is on by default** — the security and reliability posture the chart
   takes, and which parameters relax it.
4. **Parameters worth understanding** — not a restating of `values.yaml`, but
   the handful of decisions the deployer actually has to make.
5. **After deployment** — what exists, what to verify, natural next steps.

## Validation gate (per chart, before any commit)

1. `hack/guards/ensure_chart_structure.sh` — structure guard (also runs in CI
   on any `charts/**` change).
2. `cd charts && make validate-offline chart=<provider>/<chart>` — renders
   with default values and proves, per rendered document: YAML validity,
   protovalidate against this checkout's protos (build the CLI from this
   tree first so the schemas match), the explicit-triple `valueFrom` form,
   reference resolution within the chart, and `fieldPath` existence in the
   target kind's `stack_outputs.proto`.
3. **Exercise the toggle paths by hand.** The offline gate renders only the
   default values, so every feature toggle's non-default branch ships
   unproven unless you render it: re-run the harness's render + reference +
   schema checks with each toggle flipped (disabled optional resources,
   alternate tiers, populated list params) and confirm no reference dangles
   into a disabled block and every branch still validates.
4. `planton chart build <provider>/<chart>` (Platform CLI) remains the
   authoritative proof — run it when a control plane on matching schemas is
   available; the offline gate exists precisely for when it is not.
